import type {Module} from './evidence';

const example=(title:string,first:string,firstRefs:string[],second:string,secondRefs:string[]):Module[]=>[{
 id:'reading-example',label:'Reading example',title,
 paragraphs:[{text:first,refs:firstRefs},{text:second,refs:secondRefs}]
}];

export const topicDepth:Record<string,Module[]>={
 'source:mobile-phones':example('Follow the question from a phone habit to an outcome',
 'Consider two reports about people who use their phones frequently. One follows later brain-tumour diagnoses, while another compares semen measurements at a single assessment. The shared word “phone” does not make their exposure measures equivalent. Call time describes one activity; carrying location describes another. Neither fully specifies the power transmitted during that activity, the tissue receiving it or the history of exposure before assessment. Start by translating each headline into a comparison: which people, which phone behaviour, which outcome and which period? COSMOS, Rahban and Hatch then become three identifiable questions rather than competing votes on whether a phone is healthy.',
 ['cosmos','rahban','hatch'],
 'A further example is a phone with a built-in magnet. The MagSafe bench study observed a response in tested cardiac-device generators when the phone was positioned close to them. That interaction concerns a static magnet and a device response; the cancer cohorts concern reported wireless-phone use and diagnoses. It would be a category error to use the bench result as evidence that RF causes cancer, or to use a cohort finding to dismiss the device interaction. A useful phone overview therefore keeps several parallel explanations visible. Its purpose is to connect the right source record to the reader’s question while preserving the physical component and outcome actually examined.',
 ['E05-T10-R110','cosmos','million-women']),
 'source:wifi':example('Distinguish a router measurement from an overnight experiment',
 'Imagine a school report showing a higher average field when its Wi-Fi network is active. That finding establishes a change in the measured environment during the survey. It does not show that pupils slept differently, performed differently or developed an illness, because those outcomes were not measured. The sampled walkthrough average and maximum also answer different exposure questions. An intermittent transmitter can change the average without producing the same pattern in the maximum. The school survey is therefore useful for understanding where and how exposure was sampled, while the device duty-factor papers explain why network traffic matters when comparing an active test with ordinary lessons.',
 ['E06-T11-R114','wifi-pmid-21856328','wifi-pmid-23085070'],
 'The overnight Wi-Fi experiment provides a different kind of information: it compared active exposure with sham and measured sleep. Its abstract reports no significant change in subjective sleep or conventional sleep structure alongside a change in NREM alpha-band activity. Both statements belong in the answer because they describe different endpoints. The memory paper used a subset of the same participants, so its publication does not provide a second independent group confirming the sleep result. For a reader encountering several Wi-Fi headlines, grouping papers by participants and measured outcomes gives a clearer picture than counting titles or assuming that an EEG change demonstrates impaired everyday sleep.',
 ['wifi-pmid-32408065','wifi-pmid-33166026']),
 'source:5g':example('Identify which part of the 5G question an experiment tests',
 'A headline about a 5G experiment can refer to a sleep signal, skin temperature or a cellular assay. Those studies need separate translations. The sleep experiment compared defined signals before sleep and reported a spindle-frequency shift in one genotype group. A spindle is a feature of sleep EEG; the result is not itself a report that all participants experienced worse sleep. The controlled skin study instead tested a brief environmental 26 GHz exposure and monitored superficial temperature. Its absence of a consistent temperature response concerns that physical outcome and protocol. Neither experiment is a stand-in for all bands, deployment patterns and exposure durations associated with the 5G label.',
 ['5g-sleep','E02-T03-R264'],
 'The skin-cell experiment supplies another contrast. It used established human-derived cells, blind exposure conditions and temperature monitoring to examine redox responses and DNA-repair endpoints. Its reported RF-versus-sham comparisons did not show significant differences under those conditions. That is useful evidence about the tested model, even though a cultured cell cannot reproduce a person’s lifetime exposure. When comparing the three studies, retain the outcome in the sentence: spindle frequency in selected volunteers, acute skin temperature, or cellular redox and repair measures. This makes their contribution understandable without turning a physiological signal into clinical injury or extending a bounded null result into a population-wide guarantee.',
 ['E05-T09-R513','5g-sleep','E02-T03-R264']),
 'source:base-stations':example('Separate an address, an exposure estimate and a symptom test',
 'Suppose a resident can see a tower from a bedroom window and finds a symptom study online. The visibility of the tower is an address-based description, not the exposure variable used by every study. The Swiss adolescent cohort modeled fixed-transmitter exposure at home and school and compared self-reported symptoms through several analyses. It did not find a consistent association across those analyses. That finding is more informative than a generic “near a tower” label because it identifies the transmitter sources, locations and symptom measures being compared. It still does not describe the adolescent’s absorbed phone dose or every period spent away from the modeled locations.',
 ['E02-T03-R147'],
 'A blinded provocation experiment asks whether symptoms change when a specified signal is present without participants knowing the condition. Eltiti and the separate UMTS experiment therefore address immediate responses under controlled exposure conditions, rather than years of residential experience. In the UMTS study, perceived exposure and well-being were associated independently of the randomized condition. That observation supports keeping perception and actual exposure as separate variables; it does not diagnose an individual or establish one explanation for all symptoms. Read a tower-related claim by deciding which of these comparisons it invokes. Residential exposure estimation and acute causal testing can inform one another while remaining distinct sources of evidence.',
 ['eltiti','E02-T03-R018']),
 'source:power-lines':example('Read proximity and magnetic-field categories as different comparisons',
 'Consider two children living equally far from a mapped power line. A distance-based study would place them in the same proximity category, even though their complete residential magnetic-field environments need not be identical. The pooled measured-field analyses and Draper’s distance analysis therefore do not use interchangeable exposure variables. One compares estimated residential magnetic-field categories; the other compares addresses with line locations. The distinction matters when a headline replaces an observed category association with a statement about a particular property. Before interpreting a result, identify whether the underlying table uses distance, field measurement, wire configuration or another proxy for the exposure of interest.',
 ['ahlbom','greenland','draper'],
 'The transformer-room study provides a useful additional example. It compared apartment positions near built-in transformers with other apartments, using pilot measurements to support the exposure categories. Those pilot measurements were not measurements in every included child’s home. Its main matched analysis did not show a clear association, while other models produced weak elevations with substantial uncertainty. This is neither an exact repeat of the power-line studies nor a reason to convert apartment position into a personal dose. Together the studies show why a residential question needs both a physical exposure description and an outcome comparison, with the selected observational association retained separately from any proposed causal explanation.',
 ['E04-T07-R257','ahlbom','greenland']),
 'source:cordless-phones':example('Keep the handset, base station and calling history distinct',
 'A cordless system contains more than one relevant position. A handset used at the head, a base station in standby and a base station during a call describe different arrangements. The household-emissions survey measured those operating states separately. This helps explain why a single measurement beside one component cannot characterize a person’s entire cordless-phone exposure. It also explains why owning a cordless system and reporting minutes of calls are different epidemiological variables. In a study summary, look for the actual variable before interpreting a statement about DECT. The source name alone does not reveal whether the researchers measured emissions, surveyed calling or followed health outcomes.',
 ['E06-T11-R137','icnirp-dect'],
 'The tumour case-control reports illustrate the difficulty of separating cordless use from other wireless-phone habits. Many participants had used several technologies, making the reference group and technology-specific attribution difficult. The childhood cognition reports ask a different question again: reported mobile and cordless calls were related to test performance, first at baseline and later through change over time. These reports share a cohort, so they should be read as linked observations rather than independent confirmations. A reader comparing cordless-phone claims gains more by following the handset activity, study population and endpoint through the paper than by placing emission measurements, tumour odds and cognitive-task results on a single favourable-or-unfavourable scale.',
 ['E01-T01-R126','E01-T01-R127','E04-T08-R044','E04-T08-R052']),
 'source:electric-vehicles':example('Find the source of a field measured inside a vehicle',
 'A field measured on an electric bus is not automatically a measurement of the traction battery’s contribution. The Samsun case study examined magnetic flux density and RF electric fields at different positions, and cellular bands contributed to its onboard RF measurements. This means the place where an instrument is used and the source generating its signal are separate facts. A useful reading records the measured field component, the vehicle’s operating state and the instrument’s location before assigning the result to a motor, charger or communication system. The study characterizes the sampled buses; it did not follow passengers for symptoms or diagnoses.',
 ['E06-T11-R208'],
 'The charging-station paper takes a different approach: it models internal fields from a transformer at selected positions. Its authors identify missing source combinations and field validation as further work. A simulated body field can therefore answer a defined engineering question without describing every charging arrangement. The cardiovascular review mixes vehicle studies with general field experiments and has an unresolved selection-flow discrepancy, making its broad title a poor substitute for reading the original exposure source. When a claim says “EV radiation affects the heart,” identify whether its evidence is a bus measurement, a transformer model, an implant bench test or a clinical comparison. These describe different links in the proposed explanation.',
 ['E06-T11-R239','E05-T09-R560']),
 'source:smart-meters':example('Understand why a transmission peak is only part of the measurement',
 'Imagine a meter produces a clearly detectable burst during a short test. That observation describes the field while it transmits; it does not describe how often the burst occurs. The residential-device survey examined both emissions and duty cycle, allowing the peak and time-averaged exposure picture to be distinguished for the sampled communication modules. Placement also mattered because residents were not assumed to occupy the measurement position continuously. A claim based only on the largest recorded value leaves out these operating details. Conversely, a low time average does not erase the need to state what was measured, where the instrument was placed and which module was transmitting.',
 ['E06-T11-R137'],
 'The UKHSA material separates laboratory emissions, modeled absorption and measurements in a sample of homes. These are complementary descriptions rather than three clinical replications: one characterizes output, another estimates interaction with a body model, and another checks selected real settings. A household can also contain a home-area network and a utility communication module with different tasks. For a reader comparing a meter report with a router report, matching the operating state and averaging period is therefore more useful than comparing device labels. The available smart-meter material supports a bounded exposure description; it does not supply an observed change in disease incidence or a direct trial of removing a meter.',
 ['ukhsa-smart-meters','E06-T11-R137']),
 'source:baby-monitors':example('Match a monitor study to the device and the people tested',
 'The phrase “baby monitor study” can describe equipment intended for monitoring babies even when the research participants are adults. The recovered sleep pilot used a modified monitor in selected healthy adults and compared active with sham conditions. Its subjective and selected EEG findings belong beside its nonsignificant actigraphy, conventional sleep-structure and heart-rate comparisons. They do not become evidence about infant development because of the device’s intended market. Device identity matters as well: sharing a frequency band with Wi-Fi does not make the modified monitor equivalent to a home router. Read the participants, transmission system and actual outcomes before interpreting the familiar product name.',
 ['E02-T03-R227'],
 'An ordinary product description also needs the operating mode. A dedicated audio unit with voice activation, a video system with range checks and a network camera can transmit in different ways. FOPH distinguishes these arrangements, while the household survey measured parent and baby units separately. That information helps explain the exposure question without establishing a universal placement boundary. The adult pilot adds another limitation: device position varied across homes and whole-night personal absorption was not continuously measured. A complete reading therefore keeps the equipment measurements and the adult sleep comparison in separate sentences, making clear what each can contribute while leaving unmeasured infant outcomes unresolved.',
 ['foph-baby-monitors','E06-T11-R137','E02-T03-R227']),
 'source:bluetooth':example('Compare the complete wireless arrangement rather than the logo',
 'A Bluetooth headset during a phone call is one part of a larger exposure arrangement. The handset may still communicate with the mobile network, while the headset supplies a local link. FOPH describes how moving the handset away from the head can reduce head exposure compared with holding the phone there. That is a comparison between complete calling arrangements, rather than proof that every Bluetooth product has the same output or a demonstrated health benefit. A reader comparing a wired headset, an earbud and a phone at the ear needs the operating task and device positions before interpreting a general claim about Bluetooth.',
 ['foph-bluetooth','foph-mobile'],
 'The household-emissions survey provides a useful test of source matching. Its Bluetooth measurements involved a computer mouse and speakers during failed pairing attempts. Those observations cannot simply be relabeled as earbud measurements because the signal family is shared. Wearables introduce further variation: a watch paired to a phone differs from a watch using its own mobile-network connection. The selected FOPH material explicitly describes sampled models rather than a complete market survey. These examples show how to read a broad product claim in stages: identify the radio link, the activity, the transmitter’s location and the measured quantity, then ask whether the cited paper actually studied the product arrangement being discussed.',
 ['E06-T11-R137','foph-wearables']),
 'source:induction-hobs':example('Connect cookware geometry to the exposure estimate',
 'Induction cooking uses a field to heat the pan, and some field remains outside it. A measurement near the hob therefore depends on more than the appliance’s power label. The household measurement study developed an exposure model using distance from the hob centre, wattage and cookware diameter. FOPH also identifies pan placement and user position as relevant. This provides a concrete explanation for why two readings beside the same cooker can differ: they may describe different geometries or operating states. The model’s fixed-position estimate is useful for that arrangement, while the actual cooking task includes movement, changing heat output and duration that the estimate does not fully capture.',
 ['E03-T06-R135','foph-induction'],
 'The pregnancy-outcome study did not measure those detailed cooking conditions. It compared self-reported cooker use and delivery outcomes in an internet-panel cohort, with incomplete follow-up and possible reporting bias. Its weak category associations cannot be converted into a field-dose relationship using the separate measurement model after the fact. The necessary individual geometry, cookware and operating information was not collected in that outcome comparison. Reading the papers together is still useful: the engineering study explains which exposure details matter, and the cohort shows why a product-use category is a coarse substitute. They form a map of the question rather than a fixed-distance boundary for pregnancy risk.',
 ['E03-T06-R139','E03-T06-R135']),
 'source:microwave-ovens':example('Distinguish cooking, leakage testing and a repair incident',
 'The fact that microwave energy can heat food does not mean the food becomes a radioactive source. FDA describes the conversion of non-ionizing energy into heat and treats leakage and door or interlock integrity as a separate product question. This distinction helps decode an alarming oven headline: is it about the physical cooking process, a measurement outside a functioning oven, a damaged appliance or an injury from hot contents? The same product can be involved in several different mechanisms. A source about one mechanism should retain that mechanism when summarized instead of being presented as a general demonstration of every possible microwave-related harm.',
 ['fda-microwave'],
 'The occupational RF review includes a repair incident in which interlock protection was interrupted. That scenario is valuable because it identifies how an unusual exposure occurred, but it does not describe ordinary operation of an intact household oven. The review also deliberately selects high occupational measurements, rather than estimating typical personal exposure across all users. A reader comparing that incident with a household product standard should therefore retain both the task and the measurement framework. The standard addresses specified equipment performance, while the incident documents a particular failure of protection. Neither source is a clinical trial of chronic disease among people using their kitchens.',
 ['E06-T11-R168','fda-microwave']),
 'source:electric-blankets':example('Ask which generation of blankets an association describes',
 'An electric-blanket result in a meta-analysis can appear to apply to any blanket currently sold, but its scope comes from the studies included. The recovered childhood-leukaemia review based its blanket comparison on older studies rather than direct measurements of current designs. Its pooled association therefore describes those historical product-use reports and their exposure assumptions. It does not establish that a present-day model produces the same field arrangement, operating pattern or exposure history. The review also combines several exposure approaches in separate analyses, so its blanket result should be kept distinct from its results for measured residential fields, power-line proximity and wire codes.',
 ['E04-T07-R252'],
 'The physical question is also more specific than whether an appliance uses electricity. NCI lists electric blankets among low-frequency sources, while ICNIRP distinguishes electric and magnetic fields within that category. A product-use questionnaire does not independently measure either component at the body, and it may not record the settings or duration needed for an exposure comparison. This is why the historical association is worth describing but cannot supply a numerical risk for every modern design. The selected material leaves direct pregnancy-source recovery incomplete as well. That missing coverage should remain a separate open question rather than being filled with the childhood-leukaemia result or a general statement about household appliances.',
 ['nci-emf-cancer','icnirp-lf','E04-T07-R252']),
 'source:household-wiring':example('Read a home’s location proxy alongside its actual scope',
 'A wiring code, a reading at the front door and an apartment above a transformer room can all be used to organize residential comparisons, but they are different variables. The transformer study used apartment position supported by pilot measurements; it did not measure every included child’s home. The pregnancy cohort used outdoor location measurements and did not record each participant’s daily activities. These examples make the same practical point about interpretation: a location can predict some exposure characteristics without being a complete personal history. When reading a home-related study, write down the precise location or classification used before assuming that the result describes the occupant’s total daily magnetic-field exposure.',
 ['E04-T07-R257','E03-T06-R073'],
 'The pregnancy cohort also illustrates why the chosen metric matters. Front-door averages and maximum alley values gave different patterns, and the authors did not interpret the study as confirming a general magnetic-field–miscarriage relationship. Selecting only the more striking metric would hide an important part of the result. The childhood-leukaemia review likewise separates measured fields, proximity, wire codes and appliances rather than treating them as one calibrated dose. For a reader investigating household wiring, these papers help formulate a source-specific question; they do not allow a brief meter reading or a home’s address to be converted into a diagnosis or a fixed prediction of long-term illness.',
 ['E03-T06-R073','E04-T07-R252']),
 'source:occupational-rf':example('Keep a workplace maximum separate from a job-history estimate',
 'The highest field measured beside industrial equipment can be useful for identifying a task that warrants detailed assessment. It is not necessarily the exposure an employee receives throughout a shift. The occupational review intentionally selected high measurements by publication and frequency, including maintenance situations and specialized heating equipment. HSE’s task-based framework adds the work pattern, equipment information and workers with particular susceptibility to the assessment. A reader interpreting a report should therefore distinguish a source maximum, a worker’s actual task and an average exposure estimate. These descriptions can inform one another, but replacing one with another conceals the conditions that made the measurement meaningful.',
 ['E06-T11-R168','hse-workplace'],
 'The INTEROCC brain-tumour analysis uses another scale of description: occupational histories are assigned group exposure estimates through a job-exposure matrix. People in the same occupation can perform different tasks, so this approach cannot recreate every participant’s workstation measurements. The analysis did not show clear, consistent trends for glioma or meningioma across its methods; isolated positive comparisons need to be read with that overall pattern and the number of analyses. It would be misleading either to treat a high equipment measurement as proof of occupational cancer or to use a matrix-based tumour result as certification of every workplace task. The two sources answer complementary engineering and epidemiological questions.',
 ['E01-T01-R246','E06-T11-R168']),
 'source:occupational-elf':example('Understand what a job title can and cannot measure',
 'A job-exposure matrix assigns an estimated field level to an occupation or job category. It enables researchers to study historical work when individual measurements are unavailable, but it cannot distinguish every worker’s equipment, position and task. The meningioma analysis used such group assignments and found no overall association across its exposure metrics and time windows. An isolated intermediate-category elevation did not form a consistent exposure-response pattern. A useful reading therefore reports the overall analysis before selecting one category, while recognizing that within-job differences can weaken the link between the assigned exposure and what a person actually experienced during work.',
 ['E04-T07-R230'],
 'Parental occupational exposure raises a different outcome question. The CLIC analysis linked parental work estimates to leukaemia in offspring rather than measuring children’s residential fields. Its harmonized individual-data analysis and expanded literature analysis also gave different patterns, with missing histories and publication-bias concerns affecting interpretation. These are not alternative measurements of the same child’s home exposure. The occupational communication and HSE material explain why equipment and intermittent tasks remain important in a workplace assessment. Together these sources show why the words “ELF at work” need a subject attached: the worker’s tumour outcome, the parent’s history and the child’s diagnosis are separate comparisons with separate exposure pathways.',
 ['E04-T07-R237','E06-T11-R191','hse-workplace']),
 'source:mri-static-fields':example('Identify the MRI component and the person involved',
 'A description of MRI as a strong magnet captures only one component of the system. MRI also uses switched low-frequency fields and RF, which means a statement about a static field does not describe the complete procedure. ICNIRP separates these components and distinguishes medical exposure of a patient from exposure of staff. For a worker moving through a spatially varying static field, movement is part of the relevant description; a stationary field-strength value alone cannot express the task. A reader should therefore begin an MRI claim by identifying the person, the component and the activity before comparing it with a general exposure guideline.',
 ['icnirp-mri','icnirp-static'],
 'The occupational communication discusses previously reported transient sensory symptoms and gaps in long-term evidence. It is background context rather than a newly recruited worker cohort, so its discussion cannot supply an individual long-term risk estimate. This distinction is especially useful when an article cites a commentary as though it had observed a new clinical effect. HSE’s framework also separates direct effects from indirect effects such as implanted-device interference. These sources make the physical and assessment questions clearer while leaving incomplete primary long-term MRI coverage visible. They do not justify combining patient procedures, staff movement and device interactions into one category-wide verdict about MRI exposure.',
 ['E06-T11-R191','hse-workplace','icnirp-mri']),
 'outcome:brain-tumours':example('Start with the diagnosis before comparing studies',
 'Suppose a headline says that phone users have more “brain cancer,” while its linked paper concerns acoustic neuroma. That change in wording loses an essential part of the result. Glioma, meningioma and acoustic neuroma are distinct diagnoses with different study counts and patterns. The selected prospective cohorts and INTERPHONE should therefore be compared by the particular tumour and exposure category, rather than by their broad titles. A result for all included brain tumours can be useful, but it does not automatically describe every subtype. Recording the diagnosis first prevents an apparently simple disagreement from being created by studies that did not measure the same outcome.',
 ['cosmos','million-women','interphone'],
 'The same discipline applies when animal evidence appears in the discussion. A malignant heart schwannoma in a male rat is not a human brain-tumour diagnosis. NTP and Ramazzini contribute experimental hazard information that deserves its own interpretation, while the human studies describe diagnoses under their measured phone-use conditions. Reading across these streams asks how tissue, species, exposure schedule and ascertainment relate, rather than whether one paper cancels another. For a reader evaluating a causal claim, the useful sequence is diagnosis, population, exposure history and study design. That sequence preserves the informative cohort findings and the case-control concerns without pretending that the selected collection resolves every tumour and exposure scenario.',
 ['ntp-rats','ramazzini','cosmos','interphone']),
 'outcome:childhood-brain-tumours':example('Match childhood findings to the ages and outcomes studied',
 'A study described as research in young people may cover adolescents and young adults rather than following infants through an entire lifetime. MOBI-Kids examined wireless-phone use and neuroepithelial brain tumours in its specified young population. Its finding of no evidence of a causal association is therefore tied to those diagnoses, ages and exposure histories. Reading it as a universal statement about all childhood EMF outcomes changes several parts of the question at once. The result is most useful when the reader retains the actual case definition and study period, alongside the exposure error and bias that limit how precisely small effects can be excluded.',
 ['mobi-kids'],
 'The Million Women Study offers prospective brain-tumour evidence in an older female population, so it adds another human comparison without replacing the direct young-person study. Differences in age and phone-use history matter to how its results might apply elsewhere. Similarly, memory tests, sleep measures and leukaemia are separate outcomes even when their participants are children. A family looking for a childhood brain-tumour answer should be able to recognize which paper directly addresses that diagnosis and which provides context. This preserves a clear answer from MOBI-Kids while keeping unstudied ages, longer histories and different outcomes as explicit limits, rather than allowing the broad word “children” to make all studies interchangeable.',
 ['mobi-kids','million-women']),
 'outcome:childhood-leukaemia':example('Separate a residential association from a parental-work comparison',
 'The residential pooled analyses compare childhood leukaemia across estimated magnetic-field categories. CLIC instead estimates parental occupational exposure using job histories and a job-exposure matrix. Both involve childhood leukaemia, but the exposure route and timing differ. A pooled result about a parent’s occupation therefore cannot be substituted for a result about the child’s home. Within CLIC, the harmonized individual-data analysis and the expanded literature analysis also need separate reporting, because their findings and possible biases differ. This is a useful example of why a shared disease name is only the first requirement for comparing two studies; the exposed person and the exposure window must match as well.',
 ['ahlbom','greenland','E04-T07-R237'],
 'The transformer-room study adds a location-based comparison within apartment buildings. Pilot measurements supported its exposure categories, but each included child’s home was not individually measured. Its main matched analysis and other models gave different patterns with sparse highly exposed cases. This does not provide a sharp boundary at which leukaemia begins or disappears. Reading the residential studies together therefore means considering how well each proxy represents the relevant magnetic-field history and how selection or sparse data may affect the estimate. The observed higher-category association remains important, while the various study designs help explain why a personal risk calculation or a causal conclusion requires more than locating a home on a map.',
 ['E04-T07-R257','ahlbom','greenland','draper']),
 'outcome:sleep':example('Read sleep quality, sleep structure and EEG as separate outcomes',
 'A participant can report a night as similar to usual while an EEG feature changes, because these are different ways of describing sleep. The acute Wi-Fi report illustrates that distinction: no significant subjective or conventional sleep-structure change was reported alongside an NREM alpha-band finding. The baby-monitor pilot reported worse subjective scores and selected EEG changes, while its actigraphy and conventional sleep-architecture comparisons were nonsignificant. These are different signals and protocols, so their findings should not be averaged informally into one wireless-sleep verdict. A careful summary names the measure that changed and the measures that did not, rather than selecting whichever endpoint best fits the headline.',
 ['wifi-pmid-32408065','E02-T03-R227'],
 'Observational calling studies introduce a different ambiguity. The preadolescent analysis found an evening-call pattern for wrist-estimated sleep, but estimated call dose was strongly related to time spent calling. That design could not separate RF from the reasons for a call, arousal or time displaced from sleep, and it did not prove any one alternative explanation. The COSMOS sleep analysis likewise used baseline calling rather than directly measuring bedside exposure throughout follow-up. For a reader asking about a night’s sleep, identify whether the paper measured an experimental signal, a behaviour, a bedroom field, an EEG pattern or a questionnaire. This keeps the direct findings useful without attributing every phone-related sleep association to the same mechanism.',
 ['E02-T04-R174','E02-T04-R161']),
 'outcome:male-fertility':example('Follow the steps from a sperm measurement to conception',
 'A semen study and a study of time to pregnancy can reach different-looking results without directly contradicting one another. Rahban compared reported phone use with semen characteristics, while Hatch examined carrying behaviour and fecundability in preconception cohorts. Sperm concentration, total count and the chance of conception over time are related topics with different outcome definitions. A headline that changes “lower total count” into “infertility” skips the measured endpoint. A useful reading records whether the participants supplied a sample, were followed while trying to conceive or contributed sperm to an experiment, then retains that distinction when summarizing the exposure comparison.',
 ['rahban','hatch'],
 'The mouse RF experiment makes the gap between biomarkers and reproductive performance particularly clear. It reported changes in mature-sperm measures and oxidative or DNA markers, while selected fertilization and early-embryo assays did not detect significant impairment. Those assays used selected higher-quality motile sperm and did not measure natural mating, live birth or later offspring health. The result therefore cannot be reduced either to “fertility was unaffected” or to “the animals were infertile.” It describes which reproductive components were examined and where effects were reported. Reading those components alongside the human studies gives a more informative account than treating any one sperm marker as a complete measure of a couple’s reproductive outcome.',
 ['E03-T05-R267','de-iuliis','de-iuliis-correction']),
 'outcome:ehs-symptoms':example('Keep symptom experience and the tested trigger in the same account',
 'Someone can have real, disabling symptoms while the proposed immediate trigger is not supported by a blinded experiment. Those statements answer different questions. In Eltiti’s provocation study, the relevant comparison was symptoms under specified signals versus sham, including participants reporting sensitivity. The separate UMTS experiment also distinguished actual randomized exposure from perceived exposure. Its well-being findings did not establish a consistent exposure effect, while perceived exposure was associated with reported well-being. Keeping both variables visible avoids replacing an experiment with an assumption about what participants believed, and avoids treating a negative signal comparison as a judgment about whether their symptoms existed.',
 ['eltiti','E02-T03-R018','who-ehs'],
 'A cross-sectional association between tinnitus and self-described EHS has another meaning. It shows that the two reports co-occurred more often in the sampled groups, but it cannot establish the temporal sequence or identify an EMF cause. This is especially relevant when symptoms are grouped into a proposed syndrome: a shared report does not independently verify a shared exposure pathway. The historical WHO fact sheet recognizes symptoms while distinguishing their attribution. Together these sources support an account that remains precise about what was tested: an immediate signal response, an exposure perception or a pattern of symptom reporting. None of those observations alone provides an individual diagnosis or a universal explanation for everyone reporting EMF-related symptoms.',
 ['E02-T03-R054','who-ehs','E02-T03-R018']),
 'outcome:headache':example('Compare an immediate headache test with later symptom reports',
 'The acute headache experiment asks whether pain and discomfort differ during specified phone RF and sham conditions in selected participants. It reported no significant difference in that comparison. A prospective cohort asks a separate question: whether people with different baseline calling histories later report different symptoms. The COSMOS symptom follow-up found a small headache pattern that weakened after adjustment and did not follow a consistent call-time gradient. These results are not two identical experiments. One manipulates the tested signal over a short interval; the other observes calling and later questionnaires. Their value comes from the distinct sources of error and alternative explanations each design can address.',
 ['headache','E02-T03-R175'],
 'A headline such as “phone users report more headaches” also needs the exposure variable restored. Baseline call time does not describe screen activity, posture, every later call or directly absorbed RF dose. The fixed-transmitter adolescent cohort modeled another exposure source and did not find a consistent symptom association across its analyses. None of these comparisons examines every possible reason a person experiences a headache around device use. The useful interpretation is therefore outcome- and time-specific: the selected acute RF challenge did not produce the reported difference, and the prospective patterns did not form a consistent dose-like gradient. Broader individual explanations remain outside what these study comparisons measured.',
 ['E02-T03-R175','E02-T03-R147','headache']),
 'outcome:cognition':example('Name the cognitive task before interpreting a change',
 'A cognitive test may measure reaction time, accuracy, memory retention or performance on a particular interference task. Those outcomes can move in different directions and do not jointly form a diagnosis of cognitive injury. The ExPOSURE baseline report illustrates the problem: it included many comparisons, mostly nonsignificant, with selected slower or less accurate results. Its later follow-up examined change in the same child cohort and found isolated changes in mixed directions. Reading both papers as a single linked study family prevents the number of publications from being mistaken for independent confirmation, while naming the task prevents an isolated test result from becoming a claim about intelligence or general development.',
 ['E04-T08-R044','E04-T08-R052'],
 'HERMES and the Wi-Fi memory paper provide further contrasts. HERMES related modeled brain dose to memory change over follow-up; the Wi-Fi paper examined sleep-dependent memory after acute exposure. The latter’s declarative-memory result was considered inconclusive, with no corresponding pattern in the associated physiology and exploratory analyses that left chance plausible. A waking Wi-Fi vigilance experiment measured another endpoint again. For a reader comparing headlines, the first question is therefore “Which ability, tested when?” The next is whether exposure was observed or assigned. That sequence makes specific findings intelligible while avoiding a general conclusion that all cognitive functions were improved, impaired or comprehensively tested by this collection.',
 ['hermes','wifi-pmid-33166026','wifi-pmid-26600173']),
 'outcome:animal-cancer':example('Read the animal, tumour and experiment as one result',
 'The phrase “cancer in rats” is too broad to preserve the main animal findings. NTP and Ramazzini reported tumour signals particularly involving heart schwannomas in male rats, with their own exposure arrangements and schedules. A summary should retain the sex, organ and tumour type because those details determine which observations are being compared. An apparent similarity across experiments is more informative when the shared endpoint is specified; differences in other tumours or females should not disappear. The studies contribute experimental hazard evidence, but they do not assign people to the same whole-body lifetime conditions or measure the cancer incidence of human phone users.',
 ['ntp-rats','ramazzini'],
 'The Japanese arm of the coordinated replication programme is a distinct experiment, not a reanalysis of NTP animals. Its report states no significant tumour increase under survival-adjusted analysis, with limits involving a single dose, rare events and differential survival. An unresolved contradiction in the report’s cardiac-schwannoma descriptions means a zero-exposed-tumour statement remains held. This is a useful example of reading beyond a study label: independence, exposure design, survival and internal reporting consistency all matter before treating a paper as confirmation or refutation. The comparison can be described without resolving that contradiction by preserving the reported overall analysis and withholding the disputed tumour-count interpretation.',
 ['E01-T01-R257','ntp-rats','ramazzini']),
 'outcome:dna-damage':example('Track the biological sample and the assay rather than the headline',
 'A paper reporting DNA damage might examine purified sperm, cultured Vero cells or human lymphocytes. These are different biological preparations, and their assays do not measure a clinical diagnosis. The isolated-sperm RF experiment reported fragmentation and related cellular changes, with a linked correction concerning a figure and replication reporting. The low-frequency lymphocyte and yeast experiment did not find exposure-related increases in its final reported endpoints. Those results should be compared through the cell type, exposure and assay rather than described as a simple positive-versus-negative contest about all EMFs. The field category and biological model are part of the finding, not incidental laboratory details.',
 ['de-iuliis','de-iuliis-correction','E05-T09-R078'],
 'Replication also needs the correct unit. The Vero-cell report measured many cells within a small number of independent experiments; the number of cells does not become the number of separately exposed experiments. Its comet-migration findings did not directly measure reactive oxygen species or cancer. The 5G skin-cell experiment tested redox and repair outcomes under blind exposure and temperature monitoring, reporting no significant RF-versus-sham differences for those conditions. These examples help interpret a mechanism claim in steps: what was exposed, what changed, how independently was it repeated and which causal links were actually measured? That approach leaves room for laboratory findings without turning an assay response into a demonstrated everyday disease pathway.',
 ['E05-T09-R232','E05-T09-R513']),
 'outcome:female-fertility-pregnancy':example('Read the exposure proxy before linking a pregnancy finding to RF',
 'Two pregnancy papers may both say “phone exposure” while comparing very different behaviours. The Kumamoto adjunct used a text-message dependency subscale to define excessive use; the Konya report assigned phone-model SAR values from a listing. Neither directly measured fetal absorbed dose. The first also distinguished lower average birth weight from the categorical low-birth-weight outcome, which did not show a significant difference. These details change the interpretation of a headline about smaller babies. A behavioural category, a listed device test value and a clinical outcome each need their own definition before they can support a proposed RF explanation for pregnancy findings.',
 ['E03-T06-R096','E03-T06-R149'],
 'The MoBa developmental analysis shows why an apparently favourable association also needs scrutiny. Reported maternal calling was associated with some favourable early screens, while paternal-use patterns and family differences supported considering social confounding. It does not establish that RF benefits development. Similarly, the four-cohort analysis related call frequency to pregnancy duration without isolating RF or consistently measuring stress. These examples prevent interpretation from changing depending on whether a result sounds reassuring or concerning. The same exposure-matching question applies in both directions: did the study measure the field or a behaviour that travels with other circumstances? The selected observational findings remain informative while leaving the causal RF component unresolved.',
 ['E03-T06-R094','E03-T06-R109']),
 'outcome:glioma':example('Compare the full glioma pattern with an isolated high-use result',
 'A high-use category is one part of a study rather than a separate trial. INTERPHONE’s elevated estimate in the highest recalled-use group needs the surrounding exposure distribution, study design and potential recall or participation bias. COSMOS and the Million Women Study provide prospective comparisons collected before the diagnoses being followed, but also use imperfect exposure information. Their results do not become identical just because the outcome is glioma. A useful comparison asks which users the categories contain, how call history was obtained and how much earlier exposure the study can represent. The complete pattern matters more than selecting the most reassuring or concerning category from each paper.',
 ['interphone','cosmos','million-women'],
 'Occupational RF research broadens the exposure question without simply extending the phone-use result. INTEROCC assigned group estimates from job histories and did not show clear, consistent trends across its glioma analyses. Some positive comparisons appeared in particular windows or methods, with multiple testing and exposure misclassification limiting interpretation. This source should therefore be described as occupational evidence with its own measurement constraints. It does not demonstrate that a mobile-phone category represents industrial RF work, or that every positive time-window result is independent support for a common mechanism. Keeping the source of exposure visible helps the glioma overview remain about a specific diagnosis across several genuinely different comparisons.',
 ['E01-T01-R246','interphone','cosmos']),
 'outcome:meningioma':example('Notice when an isolated category does not form a trend',
 'An elevated category in a table can attract attention even when adjacent categories and latency analyses do not show a consistent pattern. The Swedish mobile-and-cordless case-control report found no conclusive meningioma association, with a high cumulative-use signal that was not accompanied by a significant increasing latency pattern. A result should preserve both observations. Many participants had used several technologies, and relatively few were truly unexposed, complicating the reference group. The practical reading task is to identify what the study considers its comparison group before treating the highest category as an unambiguous measure of a single phone technology’s effect.',
 ['E01-T01-R126'],
 'The occupational ELF meningioma analysis presents a related but distinct pattern: no overall association across its metrics and windows, with an isolated intermediate-category elevation. Its job-exposure matrix assigns group values and cannot capture every task. The RF occupational analysis also lacked consistent trends, while the Million Women Study contributes a prospective phone-use comparison. These findings are worth comparing because they concern the same diagnosis, but the field types and exposure proxies remain different. A reader can therefore state that this selected collection does not show a consistent meningioma association while still identifying the particular categories that motivated further questions. That account is more informative than either counting isolated positives or describing every analysis as null.',
 ['E04-T07-R230','E01-T01-R246','million-women']),
 'outcome:acoustic-neuroma':example('Read side-of-use findings with the hearing history attached',
 'A same-side association may appear especially persuasive because the phone and tumour are on the same side of the head. Acoustic-neuroma research has a particular difficulty, however: hearing changes can alter which ear a person uses for calls. Retrospective side-of-use reports may therefore be influenced by symptoms as well as by memory after diagnosis. The five-country INTERPHONE analysis observed a long-use same-side signal but explicitly cautioned against firm interpretation. Restoring the hearing history to the explanation does not dismiss the result; it identifies why laterality is not automatically a cleaner exposure measure than total calling history.',
 ['E01-T01-R022'],
 'The pooled Swedish report found higher odds with wireless-phone use and longer-latency categories, while combining study periods because the newer acoustic-neuroma sample was small. Many participants used several phone types, making technology-specific and side-specific interpretations harder. The Million Women Study supplies prospective evidence without an increased incidence in its reported categories, but concerns an older female cohort and relatively simple phone-use history. These papers therefore contribute different checks on the question. Comparing the diagnosis, recruitment period, exposure reporting and hearing-related changes gives a clearer account of their disagreement than treating each paper as an independent yes-or-no vote on whether phones cause acoustic neuroma.',
 ['E01-T01-R127','million-women','E01-T01-R022']),
 'outcome:other-cancers':example('Avoid extending one organ-specific report to every cancer',
 'A collection labeled “other cancers” contains several separate disease questions rather than a single residual diagnosis. The selected breast-cancer case-control report compared recalled calling with confirmed case status and an additional exploratory referral group. Its printed group totals and coding descriptions have unresolved inconsistencies, so the numerical effects remain held. This is not a reason to reconstruct a plausible result from the abstract or to turn the title into a general EMF-cancer conclusion. The useful account identifies the disease and design, describes the reported direction only within the stated limitations and keeps the unresolved numerical interpretation visible.',
 ['E01-T01-R251'],
 'The Million Women Study separately reported no increased incidence for its studied eye-tumour outcome. That prospective observation answers an eye-tumour question in that cohort; it does not repair the breast-cancer report or prove an absence of effects across every organ. Animal tumour findings likewise need their own tissue and exposure descriptions rather than being added as generic evidence for “other cancer.” For readers, the most useful next distinction is the actual diagnosis being claimed. This avoids an overly broad reassuring or alarming summary and makes clear why the current page can explain selected reports while leaving many cancer sites and exposure combinations without a direct assessed answer.',
 ['million-women','ntp-rats','ramazzini']),
 'outcome:heating':example('Separate an established physical interaction from a particular temperature test',
 'RF energy can produce heating, but the presence of RF does not specify the magnitude or location of a temperature response. The controlled 26 GHz experiment monitored superficial skin temperature during brief exposure in healthy young adults and did not find a consistent response at the tested conditions. Its unresolved pooled-comparison labeling prevents describing every analysis as null. The result is therefore a bounded observation about a particular acute exposure, not a validation of every limit or every thermal scenario. Retaining the measured tissue and duration helps a reader understand how the experiment relates to the broader physical interaction described in RF guidance.',
 ['E02-T03-R264','icnirp-rf'],
 'A microwave oven and an industrial dielectric heater provide different settings in which thermal interaction matters. FDA explains how microwave energy heats food, while the occupational review includes specialized equipment and a repair incident involving interrupted protection. The output, geometry, task and exposed material differ from the skin experiment. These examples demonstrate why a claim that “RF heats” and a claim that a particular person experienced harmful heating require different evidence. The first describes an interaction mechanism; the second needs the actual absorbed energy, tissue context and observed outcome. A measurement, a modeled dose and a temperature endpoint can each inform that explanation without being interchangeable.',
 ['fda-microwave','E06-T11-R168','icnirp-rf']),
 'outcome:tinnitus':example('Distinguish a symptom association from an exposure association',
 'Tinnitus being more common in a self-described EHS group does not by itself show that fields caused the tinnitus. The recovered cross-sectional study observed that symptom-group association while its phone-use measures did not show a relationship with tinnitus. Because symptoms and group identity were recorded in a cross-sectional comparison, their temporal order cannot be established. A careful summary therefore keeps three variables distinct: tinnitus, reported sensitivity and reported phone use. Combining them into a phrase such as “phone radiation linked to tinnitus” would change the measured association and conceal the absence of a phone-use relationship in that study.',
 ['E02-T03-R054'],
 'The COSMOS symptom follow-up offers a prospective calling comparison and did not find an association of baseline call time with tinnitus or hearing loss. Its baseline call records covered a limited window and later symptoms were self-reported, so it does not characterize every lifetime exposure or every clinical tinnitus subtype. The historical WHO symptom discussion supplies context about the reality of symptoms and the separate question of attribution, rather than a new tinnitus trial. Read together, these sources support a precise answer about the selected comparisons while leaving individual causes unresolved. They do not require denying a person’s tinnitus in order to state that the cited exposure relationship was not established.',
 ['E02-T03-R175','who-ehs']),
 'field:radiofrequency':example('Move from a transmitter description to the relevant body exposure',
 'Imagine two RF reports, one measuring a router’s field in a classroom and the other modeling absorption around the head during a controlled experiment. Both describe RF, but their quantities occupy different steps in the exposure chain. An external field at the instrument’s position is not the same as energy absorbed in a particular tissue. The Wi-Fi dosimetry paper shows that head position changes estimated regional absorption, while the school survey reports environmental averages and maxima. ICNIRP’s distinction between external reference levels and absorbed-energy quantities helps explain why these reports cannot be compared simply by choosing the larger-looking number.',
 ['wifi-pmid-32104921','E06-T11-R114','icnirp-rf'],
 'Operating state adds another step. Wi-Fi duty-cycle studies show that traffic and data rate affect airtime, while the household survey distinguishes smart-meter bursts and device-specific activities. A maximum-output test and a time average during ordinary traffic can both be correctly reported yet describe different conditions. Before using a measurement to interpret a health study, identify which quantity the study actually estimated: call time, ambient field, modeled brain dose or assigned laboratory absorption. The shared RF category supplies a physical starting point, while this fuller description determines whether the evidence matches the reader’s question. Frequency alone cannot provide that match or predict a clinical endpoint.',
 ['wifi-pmid-23085070','E06-T11-R137','cosmos','icnirp-rf']),
 'field:elf':example('Keep the field component and exposure history attached to the comparison',
 'Electrical power use can produce both electric and magnetic fields, and the two are not interchangeable measurement targets. The residential childhood-leukaemia analyses focus on magnetic-field estimates, so a statement about household electricity or an electric-field reading does not reproduce their comparison. Likewise, a mapped distance to a power line is a proxy rather than a direct record of the field over time. ICNIRP’s low-frequency description provides the physical context, while the epidemiology defines the particular quantity and category associated with the outcome. A reader needs both pieces to understand why a broad label such as “power-frequency exposure” can hide important differences.',
 ['icnirp-lf','ahlbom','greenland','draper'],
 'A workplace history and a brief residential location measurement illustrate different exposure windows within the same field category. The occupational meningioma study assigned group estimates by job; the pregnancy cohort measured outdoor locations without personal dosimeters or daily activity records. Neither records every individual’s exposure trajectory. Their respective findings therefore cannot be used as direct measurements of a particular home or work task. This is also why an immediate interaction framework, such as induced internal electric fields at sufficient exposure, should be kept distinct from an observational chronic-disease association. Understanding the field category helps organize the evidence, while the metric, timing and endpoint decide which specific question each source addresses.',
 ['E04-T07-R230','E03-T06-R073','icnirp-lf']),
 'field:intermediate-frequency':example('Compare a cooker model, an animal experiment and an implant test',
 'Intermediate-frequency research includes devices and experiments with quite different purposes. The induction-cooker study modeled instantaneous magnetic-field exposure using household measurements, whereas the charging-station paper simulated internal fields from a transformer. These are engineering descriptions, and their validation differs: the cooker model was validated in its studied setting, while the charger model still lacked field-measurement validation and omitted additional source combinations. Sharing a field category does not make the two models equally transferable to every device or person. Their value is clearer when the source geometry, included components and model boundaries remain part of the result.',
 ['E03-T06-R135','E06-T11-R239'],
 'The mouse fertility experiment and pacemaker bench experiment measure biological or device responses rather than the same engineering quantity. The mouse report described a motility-category change without directly testing mating success; the bench study examined induced voltage and pacing inhibition in selected systems and a tissue model. Neither supplies a general human disease estimate for induction cooking or vehicle charging. These examples show why the right organizing question is “What interaction was tested?” A field estimate, a sperm measure and a device response can all be relevant to the category, but their measured outcomes and translation limits must travel with them when the evidence is summarized.',
 ['E03-T05-R222','E06-T12-R016']),
 'field:static':example('Recognize a static-magnet interaction without changing its outcome',
 'The MagSafe bench experiment gives a concrete example of a static-magnet interaction. Close positioning of the tested phone could activate a magnetic response in selected pacemaker or defibrillator demo generators, and the response ceased when the phone was removed. The experiment did not recruit implanted patients or estimate how often a clinically important event occurs during ordinary use. Its finding is nonetheless specific and useful: a defined magnet, relative position and device response were observed together. A summary should retain those details rather than describing the result as RF exposure, general tissue injury or a measured population risk.',
 ['E05-T10-R110'],
 'MRI illustrates another static-field setting, with a different source geometry and additional switched and RF components. Staff movement through a nonuniform field can be relevant to transient sensory effects, while patient exposure has its own medical context. ICNIRP and the occupational communication keep these scopes separate. The comparison with the phone bench test is conceptual rather than a claim of equivalent exposure: both require the actual interaction and person or device to be specified. This makes the static-field category understandable without collapsing worker symptoms, patient procedures and implant responses into a single outcome or borrowing a result from one arrangement to predict another.',
 ['icnirp-mri','icnirp-static','E06-T11-R191']),
};

export const editorialDepth:Record<string,Module[]>={
 'question:do-phones-cause-brain-cancer':example('Turn a causal headline into a tumour-specific comparison',
 'Take a headline claiming that heavy phone use causes brain cancer. The first step is to recover what “heavy,” “brain cancer” and “use” mean in the source. INTERPHONE’s highest recalled-use group concerns a particular analysis within a case-control study, with recall and participation issues. COSMOS and the Million Women Study follow prospectively defined phone-use comparisons and did not show increased brain-tumour incidence under their studied conditions. These are informative findings, but their categories are not interchangeable quantities of absorbed energy. A causal answer must consider the complete exposure pattern and design rather than treating one category as an experiment that randomly assigned people to heavy use.',
 ['interphone','cosmos','million-women'],
 'Next identify whether the headline has changed the diagnosis or evidence stream. Meningioma, glioma and acoustic neuroma are separate outcomes; a rat heart schwannoma is another outcome in another species. NTP and Ramazzini can contribute to hazard interpretation without being described as direct observations of human brain cancer from phones. The practical answer from the selected human cohorts remains bounded to their exposure histories and diagnoses, while the case-control and animal findings identify questions that require additional interpretation. This lets a reader understand why the page gives a substantive answer without either converting one association into proof or claiming that every lifetime exposure scenario has been excluded.',
 ['cosmos','million-women','interphone','ntp-rats','ramazzini']),
 'question:heavy-long-term-phone-use':example('Check whether “heavy” measures intensity, duration or both',
 'A person who started using a phone long ago and a person who makes many calls today may fall into different study categories. Time since first use, cumulative calling and recent call intensity describe different histories. INTERPHONE’s recalled cumulative-use categories therefore need their actual definitions, while the prospective cohorts use their own reported or recorded calling measures. A claim about “long-term users” can quietly change the comparison if it substitutes one measure for another. When reading the tables, distinguish how long the person had used a phone from how much calling the study recorded and when the tumour follow-up occurred.',
 ['interphone','cosmos','million-women'],
 'A second distinction is between call time and RF dose. FOPH explains that connection quality, activity and device position affect exposure; a given amount of calling is not a fixed absorbed dose across technologies and conditions. The highest calling category can also contain relatively few participants or unusual reports, making the surrounding data and uncertainty important. This does not make heavy-use findings uninformative. It means their best interpretation is attached to the study-defined history rather than converted into a universal daily-use threshold. A useful summary states the category, outcome and design together, then explains which longer histories or more intensive patterns remain less well represented by the available comparisons.',
 ['foph-mobile','interphone','cosmos','million-women']),
 'question:brain-cancer-trends':example('Separate a population trend from an individual exposure comparison',
 'Suppose phone use becomes common while a graph of brain-tumour diagnoses changes only modestly. That comparison can be relevant to a claim predicting a large population-wide increase, but it does not identify which diagnosed people used phones or how they used them. A population curve combines age structure, diagnostic practices, disease definitions and many individual histories. The selected cohort and case-control sources instead connect participant-level phone-use information with defined diagnoses. These approaches address different scales of evidence. An incidence graph should therefore be read as a check on a specified population prediction, rather than as a personal exposure study or a complete causal test.',
 ['cosmos','million-women','interphone'],
 'The implied prediction also needs to be explicit. A claim about all brain tumours immediately after phone adoption differs from one about a rare subtype after a long interval. Without naming the subtype, expected timing and affected population, almost any broad trend can be made to sound supportive. This selected page does not supply a newly analyzed registry time series, so it should not invent a numerical trend comparison from the cohort findings. It can explain the reasoning and direct the reader back to tumour-specific human evidence. The useful question becomes whether the proposed effect would predict a detectable pattern in a properly defined population series, alongside what the individual studies actually observed.',
 ['cosmos','interphone','million-women']),
 'question:why-studies-disagree':example('Find the disagreement in the study question before the conclusion',
 'Imagine one paper reports no increase in later diagnoses among prospectively classified phone users while another reports elevated odds in a high recalled-use category. They may differ in recruitment, exposure reporting and the people represented before they differ in scientific interpretation. Prospective cohorts collect exposure information before the relevant follow-up diagnoses; case-control studies reconstruct histories after cases and controls are selected. Neither design measures every relevant dose perfectly. Comparing COSMOS, Million Women and INTERPHONE therefore starts with who entered each study, which tumour was counted and how use was assigned. A count of positive and negative papers discards precisely the information needed to understand the disagreement.',
 ['cosmos','million-women','interphone'],
 'Some apparent disagreement comes from endpoint or analysis choices. HERMES compares modeled dose and memory change, so it is not a cancer replication. NTP examines long-term rat exposures, providing a different experimental stream. The childhood-leukaemia pooled analyses also overlap in their contributing literature, which limits how independent their agreement is. A useful comparison table would put population, exposure, outcome, design and shared participants beside each finding. Once those columns are filled, some contradictions become narrower questions about measurement or bias, while others remain substantive unresolved differences. The aim is to identify which comparison could explain a result, without assuming that design labels or the direction of the conclusion automatically establish which paper is correct.',
 ['hermes','ntp-rats','ahlbom','greenland']),
 'question:ntp-and-ramazzini':example('Compare the shared tumour signal without merging the experiments',
 'NTP and Ramazzini are often discussed together because both reported male-rat heart-schwannoma findings. Keeping that shared endpoint specific helps the comparison: it is more informative than saying simply that two studies found cancer. The programmes used their own exposure arrangements and schedules, and findings varied by sex and tumour. Their agreement on a named endpoint is relevant to hazard interpretation, while their differences remain necessary for assessing transfer to human use. Neither programme followed ordinary phone users or directly estimated their clinical risk, so the comparison cannot be completed by scaling an experimental dose into a personal probability.',
 ['ntp-rats','ramazzini'],
 'The Japanese coordinated-programme report adds an independent animal experiment rather than a second analysis of the NTP animals. Its survival-adjusted analysis reported no significant tumour increase, with limitations involving rare events and a single tested dose. An internal contradiction about exposed-group cardiac schwannomas remains unresolved, so a categorical zero-tumour claim is held. This makes it a useful example of how replication should be read: identify the animals, actual protocol, pathology endpoint and consistency of the report before assigning a simple confirming or refuting label. The resulting account can retain the NTP and Ramazzini signals, explain the additional experiment and preserve the disputed detail without inventing a resolved animal-to-human risk estimate.',
 ['E01-T01-R257','ntp-rats','ramazzini']),
 'question:children-and-phones':example('Distinguish a direct childhood result from an age-based assumption',
 'Concern about children can motivate several different questions: brain tumours after wireless-phone use, memory development, sleep or residential magnetic fields. MOBI-Kids directly examines its defined young population and neuroepithelial brain tumours; HERMES studies adolescent memory change; ExPOSURE follows cognitive tasks in primary-school children. These are separate outcome comparisons rather than repeated tests of a general vulnerability score. A reader should therefore identify which age range and diagnosis or test the paper actually covers. The finding that one study did not establish a causal association cannot be extended to every developmental outcome, just as an isolated task association cannot establish general childhood injury.',
 ['mobi-kids','hermes','E04-T08-R052'],
 'An exposure measurement in a school supplies another kind of evidence. FOPH’s WLAN discussion concerns network operation and measurements, rather than a trial of children’s development. The adult Million Women cohort cannot replace direct young-person evidence either, despite studying brain tumours. These distinctions make the answer clearer: the selected childhood tumour study did not find evidence of the proposed causal association under its conditions, while memory and other questions have their own findings and limitations. Claims that children are universally more affected, or that adult results settle every child-specific question, both require links beyond those measurements. The page’s role is to show where direct childhood evidence exists and where an inference remains broader than the tested comparison.',
 ['foph-wlan','million-women','mobi-kids','hermes']),
 'question:sleeping-near-phone':example('Separate a bedside arrangement from a baseline calling history',
 'A phone lying beside a bed describes a position, but it does not specify its activity throughout the night. FOPH explains that transmission depends on connection and use, so distance alone cannot supply the whole exposure history. The COSMOS sleep study used baseline operator-recorded calling and later questionnaire sleep outcomes; it did not continuously measure nighttime phone position, screen light or changing habits. A headline about that cohort therefore cannot be restated as a direct test of sleeping beside a phone. Restoring the actual exposure variable is the first step toward deciding whether a cited result matches the bedside question.',
 ['foph-mobile','E02-T04-R161'],
 'The controlled 5G sleep experiment is more direct about its assigned signal but tests a narrower protocol before sleep and reports a genotype-specific EEG feature. It does not recreate all possible bedside-device activity. The QUALIFEX sleep analyses used still other surrogates, including modeled or measured environmental exposure, and did not isolate a bedside phone intervention. These studies can explain why source, timing and endpoint matter, while leaving the exact everyday arrangement untested in the selected collection. A useful answer therefore distinguishes a specific RF sleep finding from the broader behavioural situation of having a phone in a bedroom, and does not turn cohort calling categories into a demonstrated bedside health risk.',
 ['5g-sleep','E02-T04-R122','E02-T04-R161']),
 'question:router-at-night':example('Identify the extra claim made by a router-off recommendation',
 'An overnight active-versus-sham Wi-Fi experiment can test a specified signal and sleep protocol. The selected report found no significant subjective or conventional sleep-structure change alongside an EEG alpha-band finding. That is a direct result worth stating. A recommendation that switching a household router off improves sleep adds another claim: that the change produces a meaningful benefit in ordinary homes. The experiment does not automatically establish that benefit, particularly when its measured endpoints differ. The linked memory paper also uses a subset of the same participants, so two publication titles do not mean two independent demonstrations of a sleep intervention.',
 ['wifi-pmid-32408065','wifi-pmid-33166026'],
 'The baby-monitor pilot is not a direct router replication despite its shared frequency band. It used a modified transmission system in selected adults, with variable home placement, a small sample and retrospective registration. Child phone-use and household-source studies instead rely on reported behaviours or device presence, which cannot isolate the effect of turning Wi-Fi off. These differences show how to answer the practical question without inventing a benefit: identify the intervention, source and sleep outcome actually tested. FOPH’s WLAN material provides exposure context about traffic and intermittent transmission, while the selected human reports leave a router-off clinical benefit unresolved rather than establishing it from an EEG change alone.',
 ['E02-T03-R227','E02-T04-R143','foph-wlan']),
 'question:living-near-tower':example('Read a tower claim at the scale its study actually examined',
 'A tower-related paper can study a short laboratory exposure, modeled ambient exposure at addresses or long-term residential diagnoses. Those designs cannot substitute for one another. Eltiti’s blinded comparison tests immediate symptoms under specified GSM/UMTS-like signals, while the Swiss adolescent cohort models fixed-transmitter exposure at home and school and relates it to self-reported symptoms. Their selected findings did not support the tested signals as consistent symptom predictors, but neither is a long-term cancer study of all residents near current towers. A useful answer names the tested scale before describing the direction of the finding.',
 ['eltiti','E02-T03-R147'],
 'Distance by itself also leaves out the actual field at the occupied location. A resident’s address and a visible antenna are not equivalent to an absorbed dose or a complete exposure history. The UMTS provocation study provides another relevant distinction: actual exposure and perceived exposure were analyzed separately, with no consistent cognitive or well-being effect attributable to the tested condition. This helps interpret immediate symptom claims while leaving chronic residential outcomes to their own evidence. For a reader assessing an article about a nearby tower, the decisive first check is whether the linked study examined the claimed outcome in a comparable residential setting, or whether an acute signal experiment has been extended beyond its measured question.',
 ['E02-T03-R018','E02-T03-R147','icnirp']),
 'question:living-near-power-lines':example('Understand why a map cannot supply a personal risk estimate',
 'A map can identify a line and the distance to a residence, but it does not reproduce every magnetic-field contribution at that home or the child’s full exposure history. Draper’s distance comparison and the pooled residential magnetic-field analyses therefore address related questions with different exposure variables. Their results should not be combined into a fixed boundary on a map. The higher-category association in the magnetic-field analyses is an observed epidemiological pattern; the physical line location helps describe a possible exposure source. A personal prediction would require additional information and a justified causal model that these selected reports do not provide.',
 ['draper','ahlbom','greenland'],
 'The transformer-room comparison shows the same issue in a different built environment. Apartment position was used as a proxy supported by pilot measurements, while the study’s main matched analysis and alternative models had differing patterns and considerable uncertainty. Parental occupational evidence is another separate comparison, because it concerns parents’ job estimates rather than children’s residential fields. These examples help preserve a useful answer to the line question: there is a selected observational association in higher residential magnetic-field categories, with unresolved causal interpretation. It cannot be translated into a universal distance rule by mixing address proxies, job histories and field measurements as if they represented the same child’s dose.',
 ['E04-T07-R257','E04-T07-R237','ahlbom','greenland']),
 'question:5g-versus-older-networks':example('Decide which evidence can travel across network generations',
 'RF guidance provides physical concepts that remain relevant across network labels: the incident field, absorbed energy, duration and tissue context. That common framework does not make a historical phone-use category a direct measurement of every 5G band or signal. The selected 5G sleep experiment specifies its tested bands and protocol; older phone cohorts describe their own calling histories and follow-up periods. A reader should therefore distinguish transferable exposure concepts from direct outcome evidence. The fact that both sources involve RF is a starting point for comparison, while signal conditions, body region and endpoint determine how close the match actually is.',
 ['icnirp','5g-sleep','cosmos','million-women'],
 'The 26 GHz skin experiment and 3.5 GHz skin-cell study illustrate why the outcome matters as much as the generation label. One measured brief superficial temperature responses in volunteers; the other tested redox and repair endpoints in cultured cells. Neither is a clinical sleep study or a lifetime cancer cohort. Describing the whole collection as either proving or disproving “5G effects” loses those differences. A more useful comparison says which interaction or outcome was tested, whether exposure was assigned and what translation remains. This allows older evidence to supply relevant context while keeping direct 5G findings attached to the actual band, population and experimental arrangement rather than assuming complete equivalence.',
 ['E02-T03-R264','E05-T09-R513','5g-sleep']),
 'question:reducing-phone-exposure':example('Separate a physical exposure comparison from a health-outcome claim',
 'Moving the transmitting handset away from the head changes the calling arrangement. FOPH lists speaker or hands-free calling and headsets among ways to reduce head exposure compared with holding the handset at the ear. That is a physical comparison with a defined reference arrangement. It does not by itself show a reduction in brain-tumour incidence, because the selected cohort and case-control studies did not randomly assign those options and compare later clinical outcomes. A statement about exposure reduction remains useful when it identifies the device, body region and operating task rather than presenting an unmeasured health benefit as part of the same result.',
 ['foph-mobile','cosmos','interphone'],
 'A headset comparison also needs the complete system. The handset still communicates with the network, and a Bluetooth link adds a different nearby transmitter arrangement. The relevant question is how the combined sources relate to the body during the task, not whether the product has a wireless label. COSMOS and the Million Women Study constrain selected phone-use and tumour comparisons but cannot quantify a cancer benefit from a specific accessory. For a reader evaluating an exposure-reduction claim, this creates two clear checks: what physical change was measured, and was the claimed health outcome separately tested? The available sources support the former comparison without supplying an intervention effect for the latter.',
 ['foph-mobile','million-women','cosmos']),
 'question:phone-pocket-fertility':example('Check whether the paper studies carrying, calling or conception',
 'A person can carry a phone in a pocket without using it in the same way as another participant who reports frequent calls. Hatch’s preconception cohorts examined carrying behaviour and fecundability, while Rahban compared reported phone use with semen characteristics. These are different exposure and outcome pairs. A headline that replaces frequent use with pocket carrying, or replaces total sperm count with inability to conceive, changes both sides of the comparison. Reading the study as a short sentence—this behaviour, in this population, related to this measured outcome—makes those substitutions easier to spot before interpreting the reported association.',
 ['hatch','rahban'],
 'The isolated-sperm experiment and animal studies cannot fill those gaps automatically. Purified sperm outside the body do not reproduce intact-body exposure, and the mouse experiment’s biomarker findings did not establish natural-mating or live-birth effects. The Wi-Fi rat paper also did not quantify its delivered field or absorbed dose, so duration beside the apparatus cannot be matched directly to human pocket carrying. These sources contribute mechanistic or reproductive-model observations, with their own limits. The selected human answer remains that findings are mixed and do not establish ordinary pocket carrying as a cause of infertility; the laboratory observations should be described at the cellular or animal endpoint actually measured.',
 ['de-iuliis','de-iuliis-correction','E03-T05-R267','E03-T05-R182']),
 'question:shields-and-stickers':example('Test the path from an attenuation demonstration to the claimed benefit',
 'A demonstration may show a lower reading behind a material while leaving the complete phone system untested. The phone’s network connection, transmitted output, geometry and the measuring instrument’s position all matter to the actual exposure arrangement. FOPH cautions that shielding or harmonising products can impair connection quality and increase handset transmission. That is why a local attenuation result cannot be assumed to describe every body region during an ordinary call. A claim about a shield should first identify the source, operating task and quantity measured, including whether the phone remained connected and how its response was assessed.',
 ['foph-mobile'],
 'A claimed symptom or cancer benefit requires another link beyond the exposure measurement. The selected headache and Eltiti provocation experiments tested specified RF-versus-sham conditions, rather than branded stickers, shields or product-mediated health improvements. Their findings cannot be rebranded as evidence that an accessory prevents symptoms, and a change in a meter display is not itself a clinical endpoint. This distinction does not require choosing between all materials working and none working. It asks for a matched demonstration of the actual claim: the whole-system exposure change and, separately, the claimed outcome. The selected sources do not provide a verified health-benefit trial for a shield or sticker.',
 ['headache','eltiti','foph-mobile']),
 'question:below-limits':example('Keep the compliance statement and biological result in separate sentences',
 'A correctly performed comparison with an applicable limit can establish something about a defined exposure and measurement framework. It does not automatically explain every outcome measured in a paper. The 5G sleep experiment’s spindle finding is an EEG endpoint, while RF guidance addresses established adverse heating through specified quantities and averaging rules. Those statements can coexist without the EEG result being relabeled as demonstrated clinical injury or the guideline comparison being treated as proof that no physiological response occurred. The relevant first check is whether the reported quantity, frequency, body region and averaging period match the limit being cited.',
 ['icnirp','5g-sleep'],
 'Observational magnetic-field categories offer another example. A category used to compare childhood-leukaemia estimates is an analysis choice, not a boundary adopted to regulate exposure. Likewise, an isolated-cell exposure used to study fragmentation is a laboratory condition rather than a population safety threshold. Mixing those values with a guideline number can create a false impression that each is an alternative answer to the same question. A useful reading states the compliance comparison within its own scope, then interprets the outcome through its study design and uncertainty. This preserves the meaning of a below-limit result while leaving chronic associations and model-specific biological findings to be assessed on their actual evidence.',
 ['ahlbom','greenland','de-iuliis','de-iuliis-correction','lf']),
 'question:funding-and-trust':example('Inspect influence rather than inferring it from the result',
 'A funding declaration supplies information about a study’s support, but the sponsor’s name alone does not show which decisions the sponsor controlled. The relevant questions concern protocol design, exposure assessment, access to data, analysis and publication. A cohort and a case-control study also retain their ordinary design limitations regardless of who funded them. When comparing COSMOS, Million Women and INTERPHONE, inspect those methodological features alongside the declarations rather than assuming that a reassuring or concerning result reveals the funding arrangement. Direction of effect is not an audit of influence, and a source’s methods still need to be understood on their own terms.',
 ['cosmos','million-women','interphone'],
 'Independence has another meaning at the study-family level. Two pooled childhood-leukaemia analyses can include overlapping original studies, so agreement between their conclusions is not equivalent to two wholly separate collections of participants. Similarly, a large experimental programme and a later publication about its animals should not be counted as independent replication solely because they have separate titles. Funding, shared data and methodological similarity are therefore separate checks. A transparent account records what is known about support and study relationships, then explains the bias concerns actually relevant to the comparison. It does not need to speculate about motives or assign trust from the conclusion in order to make those limitations visible.',
 ['ahlbom','greenland','ntp-rats']),
 'guide:emf-basics':example('Translate an EMF claim into a source, interaction and endpoint',
 'Consider three everyday descriptions: a mobile-phone call, electricity supplied to a home and a magnet in a device. All may appear in an EMF discussion, but they do not describe the same physical exposure. RF guidance concerns wireless fields and absorbed energy, while low-frequency guidance distinguishes electric and magnetic fields and induced internal electric fields. The first translation is therefore from the broad word “EMF” to the source and field component. Only then does a study result become interpretable. A childhood magnetic-field association cannot be relabeled as evidence about phone RF merely because both are discussed under the same umbrella term.',
 ['icnirp','lf','ahlbom','greenland'],
 'The next translation is from exposure to the actual endpoint. COSMOS follows brain-tumour diagnoses, the 5G experiment measures sleep-EEG features, and the purified-sperm experiment reports cellular outcomes. Each can be informative without measuring the same health effect. A readable evidence claim should retain a simple chain: this source, described by this exposure measure, was compared with this condition, for this outcome, in this population or model. If any link is missing, a broad conclusion may be outrunning the paper. This chain also explains why a site can offer several clear outcome-specific answers while declining to assign a single health verdict to every field and device.',
 ['cosmos','5g-sleep','de-iuliis','de-iuliis-correction']),
 'guide:exposure-measurement':example('Compare like quantities before interpreting a larger number',
 'Suppose one report gives an ambient field at a classroom location and another gives modeled energy absorption in the brain. Their numbers cannot be ordered as though they used the same scale. Incident fields describe an environment at a measurement point; SAR describes an absorbed-energy rate under specified assumptions. The field type, units, spatial position and averaging period determine what comparison is meaningful. ICNIRP’s distinction between reference levels and basic restrictions helps organize these quantities, while the WLAN measurement studies show why device orientation and network activity add further conditions. A larger-looking printed number is not a larger exposure until the quantities actually match.',
 ['icnirp','foph-wlan','E06-T11-R114'],
 'A measurement also samples time and place. The electric-bus study measured environmental fields at selected positions and operating states, while the pregnancy studies used location readings, listed phone-model SAR or reported activities as proxies. Each can contribute information, but none becomes a continuous personal dosimeter simply because a numerical value is present. To interpret a report, ask what changed between measurements and whether the person’s movements and source activity were captured. Then keep the physical result separate from the health inference. A well-described field reading can answer an exposure question without predicting an illness, and an epidemiological association cannot retrospectively supply a missing individual dose measurement.',
 ['E06-T11-R208','E03-T06-R073','E03-T06-R149','hermes']),
 'guide:reading-risk':example('Read the estimate, interval and endpoint together',
 'An association estimate without its uncertainty interval can sound more definite than the study supports. HERMES illustrates this because the full-sample figural-memory estimate and an operator-data subset have different uncertainty. Choosing only the selected analysis changes the reader’s impression of the overall finding. The interval also needs the outcome’s scale: a memory-test change is not a disease probability, and an odds comparison is not automatically an absolute risk. Before interpreting a direction, identify what was counted or scored, which group is the reference and whether the estimate concerns the whole sample or a selected analysis.',
 ['hermes','interphone'],
 'The pregnancy birth-weight report shows why outcome definition matters even when two statements refer to the same infants. A lower average birth weight and the absence of a significant difference in a categorical low-birth-weight outcome are different findings, not mutually exclusive accounts. Similarly, a genotype-specific EEG change is not a count of clinical sleep disorders. A useful risk summary therefore retains the measured endpoint and uncertainty before discussing importance or causation. If the paper does not provide a justified absolute-risk calculation, the missing quantity should remain missing rather than being created from an isolated relative estimate. This prevents statistical direction from silently becoming a personal prognosis.',
 ['E03-T06-R096','5g-sleep','hermes']),
 'guide:study-designs':example('Choose the comparison that matches the question being asked',
 'For an immediate symptom-trigger question, a blinded active-versus-sham experiment can separate knowledge of the condition from the assigned signal. For a later tumour diagnosis, cohorts and case-control studies describe much longer histories but face different exposure and selection problems. Eltiti and the UMTS study therefore cannot be treated as short versions of COSMOS or INTERPHONE. The appropriate design depends on what outcome can be measured and over what period. Reading a paper begins by asking whether its comparison could answer the stated question, before deciding how persuasive its finding is within that design.',
 ['eltiti','E02-T03-R018','cosmos','interphone'],
 'Study labels do not remove the need to inspect the actual analysis. The ExPOSURE paper has “prospective cohort” in its title while the recovered baseline report explicitly presents cross-sectional cognitive comparisons; a later paper reports follow-up from the same cohort. The baby-monitor pilot used randomized crossover conditions but was registered after data collection, leaving questions about historical specification and analysis versions. These examples show why methods details matter more than a hierarchy label alone. A readable design assessment explains recruitment, exposure assignment, timing, shared participants and the measured endpoint. It then identifies the limitations that could materially change that particular result rather than attaching the same generic caution to every paper.',
 ['E04-T08-R044','E04-T08-R052','E02-T03-R227']),
 'guide:animal-to-human':example('Identify the missing bridge between a laboratory result and a person',
 'An animal experiment can control exposure conditions and collect tissues in ways a human cohort cannot. That control makes a reported endpoint interpretable within the experiment, but it does not make species and exposure differences disappear. For NTP and Ramazzini, the bridge to ordinary phone use includes whole-body exposure, schedule, species, sex and tumour type. A reader should name the laboratory result first, then identify which of those links would need support for a human interpretation. This preserves the importance of a hazard signal without pretending that experimental control alone supplies a personal human risk estimate.',
 ['ntp-rats','ramazzini'],
 'Reproductive experiments make another bridge visible: biomarkers do not automatically predict reproductive success. The mouse RF report found sperm and oxidative-marker changes while selected fertilization and early-embryo assays did not show significant impairment. It did not assess natural mating or live birth, and selected sperm were used in the assays. The intermediate-frequency mouse study likewise measured fertility indicators rather than a human pregnancy outcome. These are not reasons to discard the observations; they define where the evidence chain currently ends. A good translation states what changed, what downstream function was actually tested and what remains unmeasured, rather than converting every laboratory response into either clinical harm or demonstrated absence of harm.',
 ['E03-T05-R267','E03-T05-R222','E03-T05-R182']),
 'guide:limits-and-regulators':example('Identify the document’s job before applying its conclusion',
 'A hazard assessment, an exposure guideline and a product-performance rule have different jobs. IARC’s hazard classification concerns whether an exposure can cause cancer under some circumstances; it is not itself a numerical exposure limit. ICNIRP guidance specifies physical quantities and conditions for exposure assessment, while FDA’s microwave-oven material addresses a particular product framework. A reader encountering an institutional name should identify which kind of document is being cited before treating its statement as a universal clinical verdict. The authority of the organization does not make the scope of its document disappear.',
 ['iarc','icnirp','fda-microwave'],
 'The quantity being compared is part of that scope. A childhood-leukaemia study’s magnetic-field categories are analytical groupings, while an animal experiment’s field setting is a test condition. Neither becomes a regulatory limit because it appears in a health paper. Conversely, a result below a cited reference level needs the correct measurement rules and does not automatically settle every chronic endpoint. The intermediate-frequency mouse fertility experiment illustrates how a defined test field and measured biological indicators can be discussed without treating the exposure as a human boundary. Reading the source document and the study together clarifies what each statement establishes and prevents institutional, physical and epidemiological conclusions from being merged.',
 ['ahlbom','greenland','E03-T05-R222','lf']),
 'guide:home-exposure':example('Describe the room, device activity and measurement purpose',
 'Imagine comparing a phone beside a bed, a router in another room and wiring near an occupied chair. A single broadband number cannot necessarily identify all three field components or their contribution across the day. FOPH explains how phone and WLAN activity affects transmission, while low-frequency guidance concerns a separate electric and magnetic-field framework. The first useful description is therefore a source inventory with operating states and occupied locations. This is a way to understand what an exposure report actually covers, rather than a method for diagnosing symptoms or converting each appliance into a health score.',
 ['foph-mobile','foph-wlan','lf'],
 'The school WLAN survey and residential power-line studies illustrate two very different uses of environmental information. The school survey characterizes sampled fields without measuring child health outcomes; the leukaemia studies relate residential proxies or magnetic-field estimates to diagnoses. A field survey does not independently establish that an illness was caused by a room’s equipment, and a residential association does not identify the contribution of every appliance in an individual home. The MagSafe bench result is another distinct household issue because it concerns a localized static-magnet response in test devices. Keeping source, component and outcome together lets a reader understand why a home exposure question often needs several separate explanations.',
 ['E06-T11-R114','ahlbom','greenland','draper','E05-T10-R110']),
 'guide:family-choices':example('Keep each family question attached to its direct evidence',
 'A family may encounter tumour, sleep and baby-monitor claims in the same conversation, but those claims need different sources. MOBI-Kids directly addresses its specified young-person brain-tumour comparison, HERMES addresses adolescent memory change, and the baby-monitor pilot tests a modified device in adults. The last study does not become evidence about infant development because of the product name. A useful family-facing explanation begins by identifying the child’s age, the source arrangement and the outcome in the claim. That keeps the available direct evidence understandable without converting a study of one life stage or endpoint into a general developmental verdict.',
 ['mobi-kids','hermes','E02-T03-R227'],
 'Product functions also matter to exposure descriptions. FOPH distinguishes dedicated audio monitors, voice activation, range-checking signals and network cameras; its fact sheet excludes advanced bodily-function monitoring from its scope. The induction material similarly describes cookware and position rather than establishing pregnancy outcomes from a cooker reading. These distinctions help a family evaluate whether an online claim is actually about the device or task they use. They do not supply a demonstrated health benefit for changing an otherwise useful household function. The selected collection can explain what has been measured and where an outcome remains untested while preserving the ordinary purpose of the device in the discussion.',
 ['foph-baby-monitors','foph-induction','foph-wlan']),
 'guide:symptoms-and-support':example('Describe the symptom question without prejudging its cause',
 'A symptom account and an exposure-trigger test can both be taken seriously. Eltiti’s experiment compared specified signals with sham under blinded conditions and did not attribute the tested symptom pattern to those signals. The UMTS experiment separately examined perceived exposure, actual exposure and well-being. These comparisons help answer whether a defined immediate signal produced a change under the tested conditions; they do not decide whether an individual’s symptoms are real or supply a full clinical explanation. A precise account retains the person’s reported experience and the experiment’s actual comparison in separate, compatible statements.',
 ['eltiti','E02-T03-R018'],
 'The historical WHO EHS document recognizes that symptoms can be disabling while distinguishing that experience from EMF attribution. Its discussion concerns the broader clinical and environmental picture rather than providing a current review of every wireless source. The selected headache trial also tests a bounded acute RF-versus-sham question, not every reason a person feels worse during device use. Together the sources support language that explains findings without assigning a diagnosis from a website. A reader can understand that the tested immediate trigger was not established while recognizing that unmeasured timing, other exposures and individual health questions are outside the experiment. This is an evidential distinction, not a judgment about the seriousness of the reported symptoms.',
 ['who-ehs','headache','eltiti']),
 'guide:how-we-grade-evidence':example('Separate a verified statement from a completed evidence judgment',
 'Checking that a paper contains a statement is different from deciding how much confidence the whole evidence body warrants. The De Iuliis correction is a concrete example: the corrected figure and replication information must remain linked to the source even if a summary of the experiment is accurate. HERMES provides another distinction, because a full-sample result and selected subset should not be collapsed into one certainty claim. Source fidelity is a prerequisite for interpretation, while assessment also considers bias, precision, consistency, exposure matching and the actual outcome. A complete citation or a retrieved full text cannot independently establish all of those judgments.',
 ['de-iuliis','de-iuliis-correction','hermes'],
 'Some records require a specific unresolved issue to remain visible. The electric-vehicle cardiovascular review has study-selection totals that do not reconcile, and the RF reproductive systematic-review record is a protocol describing planned methods rather than completed pooled results. Neither should acquire a clinical conclusion because a page looks finished. Likewise, overlapping childhood-leukaemia analyses do not provide fully independent replications simply because both are cited. These examples explain why an evidence page separates the reported finding, its numerical magnitude where usable, the appraisal state and any scientific approval. Reader-facing completeness can improve the explanation without fabricating certainty or turning a technical content check into a human scientific review.',
 ['E05-T09-R560','E03-T05-R297','ahlbom','greenland']),
 'guide:rf-animal-experiments':example('Compare exposure delivery and pathology before comparing conclusions',
 'Two rat bioassays can both use RF while delivering it differently over time and space. A useful methods comparison records the exposure apparatus, whole-body or local arrangement, schedule, absorbed-dose description, sex, survival and pathology endpoint. NTP and Ramazzini should therefore be compared as named experiments with named tumour findings, particularly the male-rat heart-schwannoma signal, rather than merged into a generic high-dose category. The exposure description is part of the biological question: it determines what tissues and periods were tested and what kind of human comparison would require additional justification.',
 ['ntp-rats','ramazzini'],
 'The Japanese coordinated-programme report and the mouse reproductive study show why survival and endpoint selection need to remain visible too. The Japanese tumour analysis used survival adjustment and has a held cardiac-schwannoma reporting contradiction; describing every exposed animal as tumour-free would exceed the reconciled record. The mouse study reported sperm biomarkers alongside selected fertilization assays, which are different endpoints from lifetime pathology. These details help readers recognize that a methods guide is not just a list of exposure strengths. It explains which animals contributed to each analysis, what was actually observed and which unresolved protocol or reporting features constrain the interpretation before any claim is transferred to ordinary human phone use.',
 ['E01-T01-R257','E03-T05-R267','ntp-rats']),
};
